  "summary": "The health-tech sector is grappling with significant AI implementation challenges and opportunities, with major funding rounds underway even as clinical trial setbacks temper enthusiasm for some therapeutics. Ultragenyx's Angelman syndrome therapy failed its Phase 3 trial, while Teva showed promise in celiac disease, and UniQure advanced a Huntington's gene therapy toward FDA review, illustrating the mixed progress in rare disease treatments. On the AI front, health systems are rapidly deploying agentic workflows and advanced imaging tools—Elucid raised $55 million for cardiovascular AI software while Konko secured $6 million for patient coordination—yet MedCity News reports that hospitals lack adequate testing and oversight frameworks to match their AI ambitions. Significant financing activity includes Thyme Care's $125 million oncology push and Onos Health's $17 million behavioral health expansion, while data interoperability emerged as a critical gap, with Datavant's new platform targeting dramatic improvements in medical record retrieval times. Policy issues are also mounting: Trump administration pharma deals appear designed to keep Medicare prices high while lowering Medicaid rates, potentially undermining drug cost controls, and concerns about algorithm influence on vulnerable populations like eating disorder patients are prompting calls for DSM-5 revision by 2030.",
